      "title": "The Year of European Heat",
      "text": "<h3>The Year of European Heat</h3>\n<p>The summer of 2003 was unmistakably hot. People in the Northern Hemisphere experienced record-breaking heat, wildfires, drying rivers, and thousands of heat-related deaths. However, the full extent of how remarkable that summer was is only now becoming clear.</p>\n<p>June, July, and August were the warmest three months recorded in western and central Europe, surpassing previous records by a significant margin. Countries like Britain, Portugal, Germany, and Switzerland recorded national highs. Over a large area stretching from west of Paris to northern Italy, including Switzerland and southern Germany, the average temperature for the summer months was 3.78 °C higher than the long-term norm, according to the University of East Anglia's Tyndall Centre for Climate Change Research. This anomaly is considered so exceptional that Professor Phil Jones, the unit's director, has explicitly stated that the extreme temperatures of 2003 may be directly attributed to global warming caused by human actions, rather than natural climate variations.</p>\n<p>Meteorologists have previously described recent high temperatures as 'consistent with predictions' of climate change. For the region in question, the Tyndall Centre has reliable temperature records dating back to 1781. Using the average summer temperature between 1961 and 1990 as a baseline, departures from the norm, or 'anomalies,' can be plotted. Over the past 200 years, there have been at least half a dozen temperature anomalies approaching or exceeding 2 °C. However, nothing has come close to the anomaly of nearly 4 °C in 2003. Professor Jones describes this as 'quite remarkable' and 'very unusual in a statistical sense,' estimating that such an event could be expected to recur only once in 1,000 years under normal statistical distribution. He suggests that approximately 3 °C of the anomaly is natural variability, while the final degree is likely due to global warming caused by human actions.</p>\n<p>The summer of 2003 was one that climate scientists had long anticipated. Until then, global warming had manifested primarily in milder winters rather than hotter summers. Recently, the UN predicted that winters are warming so rapidly that some of Europe's lower-level ski resorts may disappear. However, the unprecedented hot summer of 2003 finally arrived, breaking records in all three months across a large swathe of western Europe. Not only were monthly averages surpassed, but daily extremes and the duration of heat spells also set new records. National records were set in at least four countries.</p>\n<p>One of the most dramatic features of the summer was the hot nights, particularly in the first half of August. The high night-time temperatures contributed to 15,000 extra deaths in France during August compared to previous years. Deaths gradually increased during the first 12 days of the month, peaking at around 2,000 a day on August 12 and 13, severely overloading medical services. However, deaths dropped dramatically after August 14 when minimum temperatures fell by about 5 °C. The elderly were most affected, with their death rate rising by 70 percent.</p>\n<p>For Britain, 2003 as a whole is likely to be the warmest year on record. However, the summer itself, defined as the June, July, and August period, ranks behind 1976 and 1995, when there were longer periods of intense heat. Globally, 2003 is likely to be the third-hottest year in records dating back to 1856, behind 1998 and 2002. However, when the records for October, November, and December are collated, it may move into second place. Notably, the ten hottest years in the record have all occurred since 1990.</p>\n<p>Professor Jones emphasizes the astonishing nature of the summer of 2003, stating that the temperatures recorded were 'out of all proportion to the previous record' and that it was likely the warmest summer in the last 500 years. His colleagues at the Tyndall Centre are planning a study of the event, which was unprecedented in terms of temperature extremes and the diverse effects of the heat. Professor Mike Hulme, the centre's executive director, notes that the 2003 heatwave will have significant repercussions across Europe, influencing how countries think and plan for climate change, much like the 2000 floods revolutionized flood management in the UK.</p>",
